That's expected behaviour of the current design, not a Redpanda problem. In Kafka/Redpanda a **group member = one consumer instance** (one `KafkaConsumer` object). Our pipeline creates exactly **one `KafkaReceiver` per topic per JVM**, so with one running app you get one member that owns *all* partitions of that topic. The parallelism you have is *inside* that member (`groupBy(partition)`), which Redpanda Console can't see.

You get more members in one of two ways — and usually you want both:

| Way | Members | Gives you |
|---|---|---|
| **A. Run more app instances** (pods/replicas) | 1 per instance per topic | HA + horizontal scaling — the "real" answer |
| **B. Several consumers per topic inside one JVM** (`consumer-count`) | N per instance per topic | Isolation of poll loops, finer partition spread, and multiple members even on a single box |

Remember: **members > partitions = idle members**. With 6 partitions, 3 replicas × 2 consumers = 6 members, one partition each — a good fit.

