## 3. Pipeline: start N receivers per topic, one `Disposable` per topic

```java
private <T> Disposable startStream(TopicHandler<T> handler, ResolvedConsumer rc) {
    var composite = Disposables.composite();

    for (int i = 0; i < rc.members().size(); i++) {
        var options  = rc.members().get(i);
        var memberId = (String) options.consumerProperty(ConsumerConfig.CLIENT_ID_CONFIG);

        Disposable d = KafkaReceiver.create(options)
            .receive()
            .groupBy(rec -> rec.receiverOffset().topicPartition())
            .flatMap(part -> part.concatMap(rec -> process(handler, rc.groupId(), rec)), rc.concurrency())
            .retryWhen(Retry.backoff(Long.MAX_VALUE, Duration.ofSeconds(1)).maxBackoff(Duration.ofSeconds(30))
                    .doBeforeRetry(s -> log.error("[{}] stream failed, restarting", memberId, s.failure())))
            .subscribeOn(Schedulers.boundedElastic())
            .subscribe(v -> {}, e -> log.error("[{}] terminated", memberId, e));

        composite.add(d);
        log.info("Started member {} for topic={} group={}", memberId, rc.topic(), rc.groupId());
    }
    return composite;
}
```

Everything else (`process`, idempotency store, DLT, ack semantics) is untouched — each member is just another instance of the same stream. `stopTopic()` disposes the composite, stopping all members of that topic at once.

